//! A persistent ordered map built on a weak AVL (WAVL) tree.
//!
//! Entries are ordered by their keys using `Ord`. Keys smaller than a node's
//! key are stored in its left subtree, and greater keys are stored in its right
//! subtree. Inserting a key that compares equal keeps the stored key and
//! replaces its value.
//!
//! "Persistent" means that an update returns a new map while any retained old
//! version remains valid. The versions share subtrees that the update did not
//! change. When the input map has only one owner, Reussir's memory-reuse
//! analysis can use its existing nodes for the result.
//!
//! ## How WAVL balancing works
//!
//! WAVL assigns an integer rank to each non-empty node. Rank is related to
//! height, but it is maintained by local rules rather than recomputed from the
//! whole subtree:
//!
//! - An empty subtree has rank -1.
//! - A node with two empty children has rank 0.
//! - The difference between a node's rank and either child's rank is 1 or 2.
//!
//! Edge labels in the diagrams below are rank differences. Here the left
//! child has difference 1 and the right child has difference 2:
//!
//! ```text
//!                [p, rank 2]
//!              1 /          \ 2
//!               /            \
//!      [a, rank 1]       [b, rank 0]
//! ```
//!
//! These rules keep every root-to-leaf path logarithmic in the number of
//! entries. Lookup, insertion, removal, and the nearest-key queries are
//! therefore O(log n) in the worst case.
//!
//! Insertion can make a child have the same rank as its parent, giving a rank
//! difference of 0. The repair either raises the parent's rank by one and
//! continues toward the root, or uses one or two rotations and stops. A step
//! that raises a rank changes the two differences as follows:
//!
//! ```text
//!          before                   after raising p
//!
//!            p:r                         p:r+1
//!           /   \                        /     \
//!        0 /     \ 1                  1 /       \ 2
//!         x       s                    x         s
//! ```
//!
//! Removal can create a rank difference of 3. Its repair lowers ranks while
//! needed, and may finish with one or two rotations. One step that lowers a
//! rank is the reverse kind of change:
//!
//! ```text
//!          before                   after lowering p
//!
//!            p:r                         p:r-1
//!           /   \                        /     \
//!        3 /     \ 2                  2 /       \ 1
//!         x       s                    x         s
//! ```
//!
//! A rotation changes a few parent-child links without changing key order.
//! For example, a left rotation changes this shape while preserving
//! `A < p < B < q < C`:
//!
//! ```text
//!            p                              q
//!           / \                            / \
//!          A   q          ---->           p   C
//!             / \                        / \
//!            B   C                      A   B
//! ```
//!
//! Only rank changes can continue through several ancestors; over a sequence
//! of updates, the average number of rank changes per update is constant.
//!
//! With insertions alone, a WAVL tree has the same shape as an AVL tree.
//! Removal may leave a non-leaf node whose rank is two greater than both child
//! ranks. Allowing this case is what makes WAVL less strict than AVL after
//! removals.
//!
//! ## Update path and rank storage
//!
//! During an update, `Path` records each node visited and the subtree that was
//! not visited. Once the key is found, or an empty subtree is reached, the map
//! walks this saved path back to the root. It repairs rank differences and
//! rebuilds each ancestor along the way. A `Path` item is the same size as a
//! tree node, so uniquely owned path storage can be reused for rebuilt nodes.
//! This avoids allocating a separate result at every recursive return.
//!
//! Unlike `WavlSet`, the map stores the full rank in each node. In the measured
//! 64-bit build with word-sized keys and values, the full-rank and parity forms
//! both required a 48-byte allocation. Storing only parity therefore saved no
//! memory and added work to each rank check. The full-rank form was faster in
//! that case, while the set's smaller nodes do benefit from storing parity.
//!
//! ## Ordered operations
//!
//! `WavlMap` provides the general map operations also available on `HashMap`.
//! Ordering adds `pop_min` and `pop_max`, which remove an entry from either
//! end, and the nearest-key queries `floor_entry`, `ceiling_entry`,
//! `predecessor_entry`, and `successor_entry`. `range` and `range_to_list` use
//! the half-open interval `[low, high)` and skip subtrees outside that
//! interval.
//!
//! `to_list`, `keys`, `values`, and `fold_left` visit entries from least key to
//! greatest key; `to_list_desc` and `fold_right` use the reverse order. `find`
//! returns the matching entry with the least key. Hash-based collections do
//! not promise any of these traversal orders.
//!
//! ## References
//!
//! - Haeupler, Sen, and Tarjan, "Rank-Balanced Trees":
//!   https://doi.org/10.1145/2689412
//! - Goodrich and Tamassia, "Weak AVL Trees":
//!   https://ics.uci.edu/~goodrich/teach/cs165/notes/WeakAVLTrees.pdf

import std::option::Option;
import std::collections::pure::list::List;

/// The internal tree representation. `Node` stores its rank, children, key,
/// and value; `Leaf` is an empty subtree with rank -1. This type is public
/// because it appears inside `WavlMap`; construct maps through `WavlMap` so
/// ordering, rank, and size remain valid.
pub enum Tree<K, V> {
    Leaf,
    Node(i64, Tree<K, V>, K, V, Tree<K, V>)
}

/// A key-value pair, as stored and produced by `WavlMap`.
pub struct [value] Entry<K, V> {
    pub key : K,
    pub value : V
}

/// A persistent ordered map with worst-case O(log n) insert, remove, and
/// lookup.
pub struct WavlMap<K, V> {
    size : u64,
    root : Tree<K, V>
}

/// The previous value, when requested while inserting an entry.
pub enum [value] InsertResult<K, V> {
    Added(WavlMap<K, V>),
    Replaced(V, WavlMap<K, V>)
}

/// The entry removed from a map, or the unchanged map when the key was absent.
pub enum [value] RemoveResult<K, V> {
    Removed(Entry<K, V>, WavlMap<K, V>),
    Missing(WavlMap<K, V>)
}

/// The entry taken off one end of a map, or the unchanged empty map.
pub enum [value] PopResult<K, V> {
    Popped(Entry<K, V>, WavlMap<K, V>),
    Empty(WavlMap<K, V>)
}

fn rank<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> i64 {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => 0 - 1,
        Tree::Node(r, _, _, _, _) => r
    }
}

// The saved search path. Each item records the parent's rank and entry, plus
// the sibling subtree that was not visited. `fixup_ins` and `fixup_del` walk
// back over this path to repair rank differences. `rebuild` then reconstructs
// the ancestors that need no further repair. Each non-empty path item is the
// same size as a node, so a uniquely owned item can provide the storage for a
// rebuilt node.
enum Path<K, V> {
    ToL(i64, Path<K, V>, K, V, Tree<K, V>),
    ToR(i64, Tree<K, V>, K, V, Path<K, V>),
    Top
}

fn rebuild<K, V>(z : Path<K, V>, t : Tree<K, V>) -> Tree<K, V> {
    match z {
        Path::ToL(r, z1, k, v, rt) => rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, t, k, v, rt }),
        Path::ToR(r, l, k, v, z1) => rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, l, k, v, t }),
        Path::Top => t
    }
}

// Repair the tree after the child in the next path item grows to rank `tr`.
// Raise a parent while it has rank difference 0 beside a difference-1
// sibling; otherwise use one or two rotations and stop. A rotation restores
// the parent's old rank, so the remaining ancestors only need rebuilding.
fn fixup_ins<K, V>(z : Path<K, V>, t : Tree<K, V>, tr : i64) -> Tree<K, V> {
    match z {
        Path::ToL(r, z1, k, v, rt) =>
            if r - tr >= 1 {
                rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, t, k, v, rt })
            } else {
                if r - rank(rt) == 1 {
                    fixup_ins(z1, Tree::Node { r + 1, t, k, v, rt }, r + 1)
                } else {
                    match t {
                        Tree::Node(lrank, ll, lk, lv, lrt) =>
                            if lrank - rank(lrt) == 2 {
                                rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                    lrank,
                                    ll,
                                    lk,
                                    lv,
                                    Tree::Node { r - 1, lrt, k, v, rt }
                                })
                            } else {
                                match lrt {
                                    Tree::Node(_, lrl, lrk, lrv, lrr) =>
                                        rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                            r,
                                            Tree::Node { r - 1, ll, lk, lv, lrl },
                                            lrk,
                                            lrv,
                                            Tree::Node { r - 1, lrr, k, v, rt }
                                        }),
                                    Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                                }
                            },
                        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                    }
                }
            },
        Path::ToR(r, l, k, v, z1) =>
            if r - tr >= 1 {
                rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, l, k, v, t })
            } else {
                if r - rank(l) == 1 {
                    fixup_ins(z1, Tree::Node { r + 1, l, k, v, t }, r + 1)
                } else {
                    match t {
                        Tree::Node(rrank, rl, rk, rv, rr) =>
                            if rrank - rank(rl) == 2 {
                                rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                    rrank,
                                    Tree::Node { r - 1, l, k, v, rl },
                                    rk,
                                    rv,
                                    rr
                                })
                            } else {
                                match rl {
                                    Tree::Node(_, rll, rlk, rlv, rlr) =>
                                        rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                            r,
                                            Tree::Node { r - 1, l, k, v, rll },
                                            rlk,
                                            rlv,
                                            Tree::Node { r - 1, rlr, rk, rv, rr }
                                        }),
                                    Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                                }
                            },
                        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                    }
                }
            },
        Path::Top => t
    }
}

struct [value] Changed<K, V> { tree : Tree<K, V>, changed : bool }

// Build one `Changed` value where the outcome is known: at a new node or a
// replaced value. Calls on the path carry that value back unchanged, avoiding
// a new result allocation at every level.
fn ins_at<K : Ord, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    x : K,
    xv : V,
    z : Path<K, V>
) -> Changed<K, V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Changed {
            tree: fixup_ins(z, Tree::Node { 0, Tree::Leaf, x, xv, Tree::Leaf }, 0),
            changed: true
        },
        Tree::Node(r, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Less => ins_at(l, x, xv, Path::ToL { r, z, k, v, rt }),
            Ordering::Greater => ins_at(rt, x, xv, Path::ToR { r, l, k, v, z }),
            Ordering::Equal => Changed {
                tree: rebuild(z, Tree::Node { r, l, k, xv, rt }),
                changed: false
            }
        }
    }
}

fn ins<K : Ord, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, x : K, xv : V) -> Changed<K, V> {
    ins_at(t, x, xv, Path::Top)
}

// Repair the tree after the child in the next path item loses rank. Lower one
// or two ranks while a difference of 3 continues toward the root; otherwise
// rotate and stop. A rotation restores the parent's rank, so the remaining
// ancestors only need rebuilding.
fn fixup_del<K, V>(z : Path<K, V>, t : Tree<K, V>) -> Tree<K, V> {
    match z {
        Path::ToL(r, z1, k, v, rt) => {
            let tl = rank(t);
            if r - tl <= 2 {
                if r == 1 && tl == 0 - 1 && rank(rt) == 0 - 1 {
                    fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node { 0, t, k, v, rt })
                } else {
                    rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, t, k, v, rt })
                }
            } else {
                match rt {
                    Tree::Node(yrank, yl, yk, yv, yr) =>
                        if r - yrank == 2 {
                            fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node {
                                r - 1,
                                t,
                                k,
                                v,
                                Tree::Node { yrank, yl, yk, yv, yr }
                            })
                        } else {
                            let dyl = yrank - rank(yl);
                            let dyr = yrank - rank(yr);
                            if dyl == 2 && dyr == 2 {
                                fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node {
                                    r - 1,
                                    t,
                                    k,
                                    v,
                                    Tree::Node { yrank - 1, yl, yk, yv, yr }
                                })
                            } else {
                                if dyr == 1 {
                                    let xrank = if rank(t) == 0 - 1 && rank(yl) == 0 - 1 {
                                        0
                                    } else {
                                        r - 1
                                    };
                                    rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                        r,
                                        Tree::Node { xrank, t, k, v, yl },
                                        yk,
                                        yv,
                                        yr
                                    })
                                } else {
                                    match yl {
                                        Tree::Node(_, zl, zk, zv, zr) =>
                                            rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                                r,
                                                Tree::Node { r - 2, t, k, v, zl },
                                                zk,
                                                zv,
                                                Tree::Node { r - 2, zr, yk, yv, yr }
                                            }),
                                        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        },
                    Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                }
            }
        },
        Path::ToR(r, l, k, v, z1) => {
            let tr = rank(t);
            if r - tr <= 2 {
                if r == 1 && tr == 0 - 1 && rank(l) == 0 - 1 {
                    fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node { 0, l, k, v, t })
                } else {
                    rebuild(z1, Tree::Node { r, l, k, v, t })
                }
            } else {
                match l {
                    Tree::Node(yrank, yl, yk, yv, yr) =>
                        if r - yrank == 2 {
                            fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node {
                                r - 1,
                                Tree::Node { yrank, yl, yk, yv, yr },
                                k,
                                v,
                                t
                            })
                        } else {
                            let dyl = yrank - rank(yl);
                            let dyr = yrank - rank(yr);
                            if dyl == 2 && dyr == 2 {
                                fixup_del(z1, Tree::Node {
                                    r - 1,
                                    Tree::Node { yrank - 1, yl, yk, yv, yr },
                                    k,
                                    v,
                                    t
                                })
                            } else {
                                if dyl == 1 {
                                    let xrank = if rank(t) == 0 - 1 && rank(yr) == 0 - 1 {
                                        0
                                    } else {
                                        r - 1
                                    };
                                    rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                        r,
                                        yl,
                                        yk,
                                        yv,
                                        Tree::Node { xrank, yr, k, v, t }
                                    })
                                } else {
                                    match yr {
                                        Tree::Node(_, zl, zk, zv, zr) =>
                                            rebuild(z1, Tree::Node {
                                                r,
                                                Tree::Node { r - 2, yl, yk, yv, zl },
                                                zk,
                                                zv,
                                                Tree::Node { r - 2, zr, k, v, t }
                                            }),
                                        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        },
                    Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
                }
            }
        },
        Path::Top => t
    }
}

// Replace a removed node with the next entry in key order. That entry is the
// leftmost one in the removed node's right subtree. `rz` records the path to
// it. Repair that path first, then continue through a `ToR` item at the
// original removal point so a lost rank can keep moving toward the root.
fn splice_succ<K, V>(
    r0 : i64,
    l0 : Tree<K, V>,
    z : Path<K, V>,
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    rz : Path<K, V>
) -> Tree<K, V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Node(_, Tree::Leaf, mk, mv, mrt) =>
            fixup_del(Path::ToR { r0, l0, mk, mv, z }, fixup_del(rz, mrt)),
        Tree::Node(rm, ml, mk, mv, mrt) =>
            splice_succ(r0, l0, z, ml, Path::ToL { rm, rz, mk, mv, mrt }),
        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
    }
}

fn remove_at<K, V>(
    r : i64,
    l : Tree<K, V>,
    rt : Tree<K, V>,
    z : Path<K, V>
) -> Tree<K, V> {
    match l {
        Tree::Leaf => fixup_del(z, rt),
        ll => match rt {
            Tree::Leaf => fixup_del(z, ll),
            rr => splice_succ(r, ll, z, rr, Path::Top)
        }
    }
}

fn del_at<K : Ord, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, x : K, z : Path<K, V>) -> Changed<K, V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Changed { tree: rebuild(z, Tree::Leaf), changed: false },
        Tree::Node(r, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Less => del_at(l, x, Path::ToL { r, z, k, v, rt }),
            Ordering::Greater => del_at(rt, x, Path::ToR { r, l, k, v, z }),
            Ordering::Equal =>
                Changed { tree: remove_at(r, l, rt, z), changed: true }
        }
    }
}

fn del<K : Ord, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, x : K) -> Changed<K, V> {
    del_at(t, x, Path::Top)
}

struct [value] DelMin<K, V> { key : K, value : V, tree : Tree<K, V> }

fn del_min_at<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, z : Path<K, V>) -> DelMin<K, V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Node(_, Tree::Leaf, k, v, rt) =>
            DelMin { key: k, value: v, tree: fixup_del(z, rt) },
        Tree::Node(r, l, k, v, rt) =>
            del_min_at(l, Path::ToL { r, z, k, v, rt }),
        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
    }
}

fn del_min<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> DelMin<K, V> {
    del_min_at(t, Path::Top)
}

// The mirror of `del_min`: remove the rightmost entry and repair along the
// path of right children.
fn del_max_at<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, z : Path<K, V>) -> DelMin<K, V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, Tree::Leaf) =>
            DelMin { key: k, value: v, tree: fixup_del(z, l) },
        Tree::Node(r, l, k, v, rt) =>
            del_max_at(rt, Path::ToR { r, l, k, v, z }),
        Tree::Leaf => core::intrinsic::panic::panic()
    }
}

fn del_max<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> DelMin<K, V> {
    del_max_at(t, Path::Top)
}

fn lookup<K : Ord, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, x : K) -> Option<V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Less => lookup(l, x),
            Ordering::Greater => lookup(rt, x),
            Ordering::Equal => Option::Some { v }
        }
    }
}

// Return the actual stored entry, including its key, when the query compares
// equal to that key but is not identical to it.
fn lookup_entry<K : Ord, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, x : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Less => lookup_entry(l, x),
            Ordering::Greater => lookup_entry(rt, x),
            Ordering::Equal => Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
        }
    }
}

// The four neighbour queries. Each descends once, remembering the last
// node that could still answer: when the descent runs off the tree, that
// remembered node is the answer. `inclusive` decides whether an exact
// match counts, which is the only difference between floor/predecessor
// and ceiling/successor.
fn floor_entry_tree<K : Ord, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    x : K,
    inclusive : bool
) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Less => floor_entry_tree(l, x, inclusive),
            Ordering::Greater => {
                let below = floor_entry_tree(rt, x, inclusive);
                match below {
                    Option::Some(_) => below,
                    Option::None => Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
                }
            },
            Ordering::Equal =>
                if inclusive {
                    Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
                } else {
                    floor_entry_tree(l, x, inclusive)
                }
        }
    }
}

fn ceil_entry_tree<K : Ord, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    x : K,
    inclusive : bool
) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => match x.cmp(k) {
            Ordering::Greater => ceil_entry_tree(rt, x, inclusive),
            Ordering::Less => {
                let below = ceil_entry_tree(l, x, inclusive);
                match below {
                    Option::Some(_) => below,
                    Option::None => Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
                }
            },
            Ordering::Equal =>
                if inclusive {
                    Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
                } else {
                    ceil_entry_tree(rt, x, inclusive)
                }
        }
    }
}

// Ascending entries of `[low, high)`, prepended onto `acc`. Subtrees that
// cannot hold a key in the window are never entered, so this costs
// O(log n + k) for k reported entries rather than O(n).
fn range_acc<K : Ord, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    low : K,
    high : K,
    acc : List<Entry<K, V>>
) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => acc,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => {
            if k < low {
                range_acc(rt, low, high, acc)
            } else {
                if !(k < high) {
                    range_acc(l, low, high, acc)
                } else {
                    range_acc(
                        l,
                        low,
                        high,
                        List::Cons {
                            Entry { key: k, value: v },
                            range_acc(rt, low, high, acc)
                        }
                    )
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

fn min_tree<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, Tree::Leaf, k, v, _) =>
            Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } },
        Tree::Node(_, l, _, _, _) => min_tree(l)
    }
}

fn max_tree<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, _, k, v, Tree::Leaf) =>
            Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } },
        Tree::Node(_, _, _, _, rt) => max_tree(rt)
    }
}

fn to_list_acc<K, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    acc : List<Entry<K, V>>
) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => acc,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => to_list_acc(
            l,
            List::Cons { Entry { key: k, value: v }, to_list_acc(rt, acc) }
        )
    }
}

fn fold_tree<U, K, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    initial : U,
    f : (U, K, V) -> U
) -> U {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => initial,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) =>
            fold_tree(rt, f(fold_tree(l, initial, f), k, v), f)
    }
}

fn depth_tree<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>) -> u64 {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => 0,
        Tree::Node(_, l, _, _, rt) => {
            let dl = depth_tree(l);
            let dr = depth_tree(rt);
            1 + (if dl < dr { dr } else { dl })
        }
    }
}

fn keys_acc<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, acc : List<K>) -> List<K> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => acc,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, _, rt) =>
            keys_acc(l, List::Cons { k, keys_acc(rt, acc) })
    }
}

fn values_acc<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, acc : List<V>) -> List<V> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => acc,
        Tree::Node(_, l, _, v, rt) =>
            values_acc(l, List::Cons { v, values_acc(rt, acc) })
    }
}

fn to_list_desc_acc<K, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    acc : List<Entry<K, V>>
) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => acc,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => to_list_desc_acc(
            rt,
            List::Cons { Entry { key: k, value: v }, to_list_desc_acc(l, acc) }
        )
    }
}

fn fold_right_tree<U, K, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    initial : U,
    f : (K, V, U) -> U
) -> U {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => initial,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) =>
            fold_right_tree(l, f(k, v, fold_right_tree(rt, initial, f)), f)
    }
}

// These searches stop as soon as the answer is known, which a fold cannot do.
// The `||` and `&&` expressions only visit another subtree while its result
// can still change the answer.
// `find_tree` keeps ascending order, so the entry it returns is the one
// with the least key rather than an arbitrary one.
fn any_tree<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> bool {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => false,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => predicate(k, v)
            || any_tree(l, predicate)
            || any_tree(rt, predicate)
    }
}

fn all_tree<K, V>(t : Tree<K, V>, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> bool {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => true,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => predicate(k, v)
            && all_tree(l, predicate)
            && all_tree(rt, predicate)
    }
}

fn find_tree<K, V>(
    t : Tree<K, V>,
    predicate : (K, V) -> bool
) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
    match t {
        Tree::Leaf => Option::None,
        Tree::Node(_, l, k, v, rt) => {
            let left = find_tree(l, predicate);
            match left {
                Option::Some(_) => left,
                Option::None =>
                    if predicate(k, v) {
                        Option::Some { Entry { key: k, value: v } }
                    } else {
                        find_tree(rt, predicate)
                    }
            }
        }
    }
}

fn from_list_impl<K : Ord, V>(
    values : List<Entry<K, V>>,
    acc : WavlMap<K, V>
) -> WavlMap<K, V> {
    match values {
        List::Nil => acc,
        List::Cons(entry, rest) =>
            from_list_impl(rest, acc.insert(entry.key, entry.value))
    }
}

impl<K : Ord, V> WavlMap<K, V> {
    /// Creates an empty map. O(1).
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        WavlMap { size: 0, root: Tree::Leaf }
    }

    /// Creates a one-entry map. O(1).
    pub fn singleton(key : K, value : V) -> Self {
        WavlMap::new().insert(key, value)
    }

    /// Removes every entry. O(1).
    pub fn clear(self : Self) -> Self {
        WavlMap { size: 0, root: Tree::Leaf }
    }

    /// Returns the number of entries. O(1).
    pub fn len(self : Self) -> u64 {
        self.size
    }

    /// Returns true when the map contains no entries. O(1).
    pub fn is_empty(self : Self) -> bool {
        self.size == 0
    }

    /// Returns the number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path, zero
    /// for an empty map. The WAVL rank rules bound this by `2·log2(n) + 1`;
    /// tests use this bound to check that updates preserve balance. O(n).
    pub fn depth(self : Self) -> u64 {
        depth_tree(self.root)
    }

    /// Adds or replaces the entry for `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn insert(self : Self, key : K, value : V) -> Self {
        // Decompose before mutating: a live `self` across `ins` would keep
        // the root shared, forcing a path copy per update.
        let size = self.size;
        let root = self.root;
        let r = ins(root, key, value);
        WavlMap {
            size: if r.changed { size + 1 } else { size },
            root: r.tree
        }
    }

    /// Adds or replaces an entry and reports the previous value. O(log n).
    pub fn insert_result(self : Self, key : K, value : V) -> InsertResult<K, V> {
        match self.get(key) {
            Option::Some(previous) =>
                InsertResult::Replaced { previous, self.insert(key, value) },
            Option::None => InsertResult::Added { self.insert(key, value) }
        }
    }

    /// Combines an existing value with `value`, or inserts `value` when the
    /// key is absent. `combine` receives the existing value first. O(log n).
    pub fn insert_with(
        self : Self,
        key : K,
        value : V,
        combine : (V, V) -> V
    ) -> Self {
        match self.get(key) {
            Option::Some(current) => self.insert(key, combine(current, value)),
            Option::None => self.insert(key, value)
        }
    }

    /// Removes the entry for `key` when present. O(log n).
    pub fn remove(self : Self, key : K) -> Self {
        let size = self.size;
        let root = self.root;
        let r = del(root, key);
        WavlMap {
            size: if r.changed { size - 1 } else { size },
            root: r.tree
        }
    }

    /// Removes and returns the stored entry for `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn remove_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> RemoveResult<K, V> {
        match lookup_entry(self.root, key) {
            Option::Some(entry) =>
                RemoveResult::Removed { entry, self.remove(key) },
            Option::None => RemoveResult::Missing { self }
        }
    }

    /// Removes and returns the entry with the least key. O(log n).
    pub fn pop_min(self : Self) -> PopResult<K, V> {
        let size = self.size;
        let root = self.root;
        if size == 0 {
            PopResult::Empty { WavlMap { size, root } }
        } else {
            let least = del_min(root);
            PopResult::Popped {
                Entry { key: least.key, value: least.value },
                WavlMap { size: size - 1, root: least.tree }
            }
        }
    }

    /// Removes and returns the entry with the greatest key. O(log n).
    pub fn pop_max(self : Self) -> PopResult<K, V> {
        let size = self.size;
        let root = self.root;
        if size == 0 {
            PopResult::Empty { WavlMap { size, root } }
        } else {
            let greatest = del_max(root);
            PopResult::Popped {
                Entry { key: greatest.key, value: greatest.value },
                WavlMap { size: size - 1, root: greatest.tree }
            }
        }
    }

    /// Applies `f` to an existing value, leaving a missing key unchanged.
    /// O(log n).
    pub fn adjust(self : Self, key : K, f : V -> V) -> Self {
        match self.get(key) {
            Option::Some(value) => self.insert(key, f(value)),
            Option::None => self
        }
    }

    /// Updates an existing value; returning `None` from `f` removes the
    /// entry. A missing key is left unchanged. O(log n).
    pub fn update(self : Self, key : K, f : V -> Option<V>) -> Self {
        match self.get(key) {
            Option::None => self,
            Option::Some(value) => match f(value) {
                Option::Some(next) => self.insert(key, next),
                Option::None => self.remove(key)
            }
        }
    }

    /// Alters the optional value for `key`; `Some` inserts or replaces and
    /// `None` removes. O(log n).
    pub fn alter(self : Self, key : K, f : Option<V> -> Option<V>) -> Self {
        match f(self.get(key)) {
            Option::Some(value) => self.insert(key, value),
            Option::None => self.remove(key)
        }
    }

    /// Returns the value stored for `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn get(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<V> {
        lookup(self.root, key)
    }

    /// Returns the actual stored key and value equal to `key`. The returned key
    /// can differ from the query when they compare equal but are not
    /// identical. O(log n).
    pub fn get_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        lookup_entry(self.root, key)
    }

    /// Returns true if an entry exists for `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn contains_key(self : Self, key : K) -> bool {
        self.get(key).is_some()
    }

    /// Returns the entry with the least key. O(log n).
    pub fn min(self : Self) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        min_tree(self.root)
    }

    /// Returns the entry with the greatest key. O(log n).
    pub fn max(self : Self) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        max_tree(self.root)
    }

    /// Returns the entry whose key is greatest among those at most `key`.
    /// O(log n).
    pub fn floor_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        floor_entry_tree(self.root, key, true)
    }

    /// Returns the entry whose key is least among those at least `key`.
    /// O(log n).
    pub fn ceiling_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        ceil_entry_tree(self.root, key, true)
    }

    /// Returns the entry whose key is greatest among those strictly below
    /// `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn predecessor_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        floor_entry_tree(self.root, key, false)
    }

    /// Returns the entry whose key is least among those strictly above
    /// `key`. O(log n).
    pub fn successor_entry(self : Self, key : K) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        ceil_entry_tree(self.root, key, false)
    }

    /// Returns the entries keyed by `[low, high)` in ascending key order,
    /// visiting only subtrees that can contain a matching key. O(log n + k)
    /// for k returned entries.
    pub fn range_to_list(self : Self, low : K, high : K) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
        range_acc(self.root, low, high, List::Nil)
    }

    /// Returns the entries keyed by `[low, high)` as a map.
    /// O(k log k + log n).
    pub fn range(self : Self, low : K, high : K) -> Self {
        WavlMap::from_list(self.range_to_list(low, high))
    }

    /// Returns the entries in ascending key order. O(n).
    pub fn to_list(self : Self) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
        to_list_acc(self.root, List::Nil)
    }

    /// Returns the entries in descending key order. O(n).
    pub fn to_list_desc(self : Self) -> List<Entry<K, V>> {
        to_list_desc_acc(self.root, List::Nil)
    }

    /// Returns the keys in ascending order. O(n).
    pub fn keys(self : Self) -> List<K> {
        keys_acc(self.root, List::Nil)
    }

    /// Returns the values ordered by their keys. O(n).
    pub fn values(self : Self) -> List<V> {
        values_acc(self.root, List::Nil)
    }

    /// Folds the entries in ascending key order. O(n).
    pub fn fold_left<U>(self : Self, initial : U, f : (U, K, V) -> U) -> U {
        fold_tree(self.root, initial, f)
    }

    /// Folds the entries in descending key order, each entry seeing the
    /// result for every greater key. O(n).
    pub fn fold_right<U>(self : Self, initial : U, f : (K, V, U) -> U) -> U {
        fold_right_tree(self.root, initial, f)
    }

    /// Creates a map from entries, later duplicates replacing earlier ones. O(n log n).
    pub fn from_list(values : List<Entry<K, V>>) -> Self {
        from_list_impl(values, WavlMap::new())
    }

    /// Transforms every value while retaining its key. O(n log n).
    pub fn map_values<U>(self : Self, f : V -> U) -> WavlMap<K, U> {
        let initial : WavlMap<K, U> = WavlMap::new();
        self.fold_left(initial, |map, key, value| map.insert(key, f(value)))
    }

    /// Transforms entries into a map with possibly different key and value
    /// types. Later transformed duplicates replace earlier values.
    /// O(n log n).
    pub fn map_entries<L : Ord, U>(
        self : Self,
        f : (K, V) -> Entry<L, U>
    ) -> WavlMap<L, U> {
        let initial : WavlMap<L, U> = WavlMap::new();
        self.fold_left(initial, |map, key, value| {
            let entry = f(key, value);
            map.insert(entry.key, entry.value)
        })
    }

    /// Transforms every key while retaining its value. Later transformed
    /// duplicates replace earlier values. O(n log n).
    pub fn map_keys<L : Ord>(self : Self, f : K -> L) -> WavlMap<L, V> {
        self.map_entries(|key, value| Entry { key: f(key), value })
    }

    /// Keeps entries for which `predicate` returns true. O(n log n).
    pub fn filter(self : Self, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> Self {
        let initial : WavlMap<K, V> = WavlMap::new();
        self.fold_left(initial, |map, key, value| {
            if predicate(key, value) { map.insert(key, value) } else { map }
        })
    }

    /// Transforms retained values and discards entries mapped to `None`.
    /// O(n log n).
    pub fn filter_map<U>(self : Self, f : (K, V) -> Option<U>) -> WavlMap<K, U> {
        let initial : WavlMap<K, U> = WavlMap::new();
        self.fold_left(initial, |map, key, value| match f(key, value) {
            Option::Some(next) => map.insert(key, next),
            Option::None => map
        })
    }

    /// Counts entries satisfying `predicate`. O(n).
    pub fn count_if(self : Self, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> u64 {
        self.fold_left(0, |count, key, value| {
            if predicate(key, value) { count + 1 } else { count }
        })
    }

    /// Returns true if any entry satisfies `predicate`, stopping at the
    /// first match. O(n) worst case.
    pub fn any(self : Self, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> bool {
        any_tree(self.root, predicate)
    }

    /// Returns true if every entry satisfies `predicate`, stopping at the
    /// first counterexample. O(n) worst case.
    pub fn all(self : Self, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> bool {
        all_tree(self.root, predicate)
    }

    /// Returns the matching entry with the least key, or `None`. Unlike the
    /// hash containers, the ordering makes this choice deterministic.
    /// O(n) worst case.
    pub fn find(self : Self, predicate : (K, V) -> bool) -> Option<Entry<K, V>> {
        find_tree(self.root, predicate)
    }

    /// Inserts every entry from `other`; values from `other` win on
    /// duplicate keys. O(m log(n + m)).
    pub fn union(self : Self, other : Self) -> Self {
        other.fold_left(self, |map, key, value| map.insert(key, value))
    }

    /// Unions two maps and combines duplicate values, receiving the value
    /// from this map first and `other` second. O(m log(n + m)).
    pub fn union_with(self : Self, other : Self, combine : (V, V) -> V) -> Self {
        other.fold_left(self, |map, key, right| match map.get(key) {
            Option::Some(left) => map.insert(key, combine(left, right)),
            Option::None => map.insert(key, right)
        })
    }

    /// Keeps entries from this map whose keys also occur in `other`, with
    /// this map's values. O(n log m).
    pub fn intersection<W>(self : Self, other : WavlMap<K, W>) -> Self {
        self.filter(|key, ignored_value| other.contains_key(key))
    }

    /// Keeps shared keys and combines their values, using this map's stored
    /// keys. O(n log m).
    pub fn intersection_with<W, U>(
        self : Self,
        other : WavlMap<K, W>,
        combine : (V, W) -> U
    ) -> WavlMap<K, U> {
        let initial : WavlMap<K, U> = WavlMap::new();
        self.fold_left(initial, |map, key, value| match other.get(key) {
            Option::Some(right) => map.insert(key, combine(value, right)),
            Option::None => map
        })
    }

    /// Removes every key occurring in `other`. O(m log n).
    pub fn difference<W>(self : Self, other : WavlMap<K, W>) -> Self {
        other.fold_left(self, |map, key, ignored_value| map.remove(key))
    }

    /// Returns true when the maps share no key. O(min(n, m) log max(n, m)).
    pub fn is_disjoint<W>(self : Self, other : WavlMap<K, W>) -> bool {
        if self.len() <= other.len() {
            self.all(|key, ignored_value| !other.contains_key(key))
        } else {
            other.all(|key, ignored_value| !self.contains_key(key))
        }
    }

    /// Returns true when every entry in this map has a matching key and
    /// value accepted by `predicate` in `other`. O(n log m). When this map is
    /// larger, the size test returns immediately.
    pub fn is_submap_by<W>(
        self : Self,
        other : WavlMap<K, W>,
        predicate : (V, W) -> bool
    ) -> bool {
        self.len() <= other.len() && self.all(|key, value| match other.get(key) {
            Option::Some(right) => predicate(value, right),
            Option::None => false
        })
    }

    /// Returns true when this map is smaller than `other` and every entry has
    /// a matching key whose two values are accepted by `predicate`.
    /// O(n log m).
    pub fn is_proper_submap_by<W>(
        self : Self,
        other : WavlMap<K, W>,
        predicate : (V, W) -> bool
    ) -> bool {
        self.len() < other.len() && self.is_submap_by(other, predicate)
    }
}

impl<K : Ord, V : PartialEq> WavlMap<K, V> {
    /// Returns true when both maps contain equal values for exactly the same
    /// keys. O(n log n).
    pub fn equals(self : Self, other : Self) -> bool {
        self.len() == other.len()
            && self.is_submap_by(other, |left, right| left == right)
    }

    /// Returns true when every entry in this map also occurs in `other`.
    /// O(n log m).
    pub fn is_submap(self : Self, other : Self) -> bool {
        self.is_submap_by(other, |left, right| left == right)
    }

    /// Returns true when this map is a strict submap of `other`. O(n log m).
    pub fn is_proper_submap(self : Self, other : Self) -> bool {
        self.is_proper_submap_by(other, |left, right| left == right)
    }

    /// Returns true if any entry has a value equal to `value`. O(n).
    pub fn contains_value(self : Self, value : V) -> bool {
        self.any(|ignored_key, stored| stored == value)
    }
}

impl<K : Ord, V : PartialEq> PartialEq for WavlMap<K, V> {
    fn eq(self : Self, other : Self) -> bool {
        self.equals(other)
    }
}

impl<K : Ord, V : Eq> Eq for WavlMap<K, V> {}
